  "rules": "MINI CHESS - Gardner 5x5 (5x5 board).\n\nOVERVIEW\nChess on a small 5x5 board with simplified rules. Player 0 is WHITE (uppercase letters) and moves\nfirst; Player 1 is BLACK (lowercase letters). Players alternate, one move per turn; passing is not\nallowed.\n\nBOARD AND COORDINATES\nFiles (columns) are letters a..e from left to right as seen by White; ranks (rows) are numbers\n1..5 from White's side (bottom) to Black's side (top). A square is file + rank, e.g. \"c3\".\nLetters on the board: K/k king, Q/q queen, R/r rook, B/b bishop, N/n knight, P/p pawn; \".\" = empty.\nPieces used: king, queen, rook, bishop, knight, pawn.\nStarting position:\nWhite (uppercase) on rank 1: a1 Rook, b1 Knight, c1 Bishop, d1 Queen, e1 King; White pawns on\na2-e2. Black (lowercase) mirrors it: a5 rook, b5 knight, c5 bishop, d5 queen, e5 king; pawns on\na4-e4. Only rank 3 is empty at the start.\n    a b c d e\n 5  r n b q k  5\n 4  p p p p p  4\n 3  . . . . .  3\n 2  P P P P P  2\n 1  R N B Q K  1\n    a b c d e\n\nHOW PIECES MOVE (standard chess)\n- King: one square in any direction (8 directions).\n- Queen: any number of empty squares in a straight line: horizontally, vertically or diagonally.\n- Rook: any number of empty squares horizontally or vertically.\n- Bishop: any number of empty squares diagonally.\n- Knight: an \"L\" jump: two squares in one direction then one square sideways; it jumps over pieces.\n- Pawn: moves ONE square straight forward (White up toward rank 5, Black down toward rank 1) onto\n  an EMPTY square, and captures ONE square diagonally forward. A pawn can never move backwards or\n  sideways and cannot capture straight ahead.\n- Capturing: moving onto a square occupied by an enemy piece removes that piece. You can never move\n  onto your own piece. Sliding pieces cannot jump over pieces.\nSIMPLIFICATIONS: no castling, no en passant, NO pawn double step (pawns always move one square), and\na pawn that reaches the last rank (rank 5 for White, rank 1 for Black) is automatically promoted\nto a QUEEN (always a queen).\n\nCHECK AND CHECKMATE\nA king that is attacked by an enemy piece is \"in check\". You may never make a move that leaves your\nown king in check (moves that do are illegal). If the side to move is in check and has no legal move,\nthat is CHECKMATE and the side to move loses. If the side to move is NOT in check but has no legal\nmove, that is STALEMATE: a draw.\n\nACTION FORMAT\n    <from-square><to-square>\nExamples:  \"b2b3\" (pawn one step forward)   \"b1c3\" (knight jump)   \"c3d4\" (captures whatever enemy\npiece stands on d4)   \"a4a5\" (a white pawn reaching the last rank: becomes a queen).\nPromotion is automatic; you may add \"q\" (\"a4a5q\") but other promotion letters are illegal.\nUpper/lower case, spaces and a \"-\" between the squares are ignored (\"B2-B3\" works), and an optional\npiece letter in front is accepted when it matches the moving piece (\"Nb1c3\"). Short algebraic\nnotation like \"Nc3\" or \"e4\" is NOT accepted: always give both squares. Anything else (wrong piece,\nleaves your king in check, not a legal move for that piece...) is illegal (first time: a warning and\nthe same turn again; second time: you lose the game).\nWhen there are at most 50 legal moves the arena sends you the complete list; otherwise the list is\nomitted (null) and you must work out a legal move yourself.\n\nWHAT YOU OBSERVE\nThe board (rank 5 at the top, White's rank 1 at the bottom), side to move, whether you are in\ncheck, material count, captured pieces and the full move list. Example: the starting position above.\n\nHOW THE GAME ENDS AND IS SCORED\n- Checkmate: the side that delivered mate wins (score 1, loser 0).\n- Stalemate, or only the two kings left: draw (0.5 each).\n- MOVE CAP: after 40 moves by each side (80 moves in total) the game stops. Then the\n  player with more MATERIAL on the board wins, counting queen 9, rook 5, bishop 3, knight 3, pawn 1\n  (kings not counted). Equal material = draw. So capturing pieces matters even without a mate.\n",
